# The post-storm coherence case, run by the driver AFTER all 10 HUPs have
# landed and the last reload has been fully absorbed. It proves the server
# came back HEALTHY: the final worker serves a correct 200, leaks no
# descriptor, and logs nothing on the error path in the window around this
# request.
#
# The pid oracle is STRICT (no pid_may_change), same rationale as
# backend-reload-inflight's post-reload.rule: by the time this file runs, a
# single post-storm worker is serving, so both of the case's probe snapshots
# see the SAME worker and "same worker pid" is the stronger, true assertion.
# The reload-survival claims this scenario exists to make are the driver's
# own oracles, which straddle the ten signals; this leg only proves the
# server came back healthy afterwards.

name    the final worker serves cleanly after the 10-HUP storm
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   fds == 0
no_error_log  \[error\]
